    "available_at": "2026-11-13T17:30:12.279902+00:00",
    "body": "The UK will buy three counter-mine \"motherships\" from Norway under a new agreement. (UK Ministry of Defence)\nMILAN — The UK this week formalized plans to purchase three new mine-hunting “motherships” that can deploy unmanned mine countermeasure assets, as part of a defense cooperation agreement with Norway.\nUnder the UK-Norway Lunna House Agreement , signed last December, the British government announced its intention to join the Norwegian P1118 Standardized Vessel Program to develop offshore support vessels as motherships for autonomous mine-hunting systems.\nThe decision, for which £90 million ($119.1 million) has been allocated, was formalized on Thursday by British Defense Secretary Wes Streeting and his Norwegian counterpart, Tore O. Sandvik, at the Atlantic Future Forum in Oslo.\n“This deal strengthens our ability to protect freedom of navigation, which is critical to our security and our economy …[We are] using the biggest sustained increase in defense spending since the Cold War to put the latest modern technology in the hands of our sailors,” Streeting said.\nThe British Ministry of Defense said the vessels, built on the same family as Norway’s naval version of the P1118 design, will help keep sailors “further from danger” and, as a result, support “anti-submarine warfare capabilities on NATO’s northern flank.”\nThe first ship is expected to arrive in 2031, and the deal includes an option for London to order three more.\nThe threat posed by naval mines, especially in the Strait of Hormuz, has surged since the start of the Iran war in February, when Tehran launched mine-laying operations to restrict navigation of the waterway. In Thursday’s announcement, the British MoD called these operations “illegal.” It said they caused “major disruption to the global trade in energy,” adding that the new mine-hunting capabilities will help the UK stay ahead of this threat.\nBreaking Defense Video\nNATO nations have a missile crisis. How did it happen, and can it be fixed?\nWatch Now »\nA Pentagon impeachment showdown, plus defense funding in limbo\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nThe push to give dismounted forces direct access to LEO and MEO networks [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nThe Pentagon wants a massive new military base. Where will it go?\nWatch Now »\nWhat now? Budget fight, Hegseth impeachment on deck for defense-focused lawmakers after midterms\nWatch Now »\nThe defense show circuit keeps rolling, plus Jimmy Stewart goes to war\nWatch Now »\nQatar’s domestic weapons, Turkey’s drone arsenal and the Mecca pact’s next test\nWatch Now »\n3 congressional races that matter for defense\nWatch Now »\nHow General Atomics views Europe’s unmanned future\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nA more flexible way to defend maneuver forces from drones that doesn’t tie them down [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
